; Let's define the circle with center g that passes through point a
; Since g is the midpoint of ab, this circle will also pass through b
:extrafuns ((C Circle))
:assumption (center g C)
:assumption (onc a C)
:assumption (onc b C)

; Since g is the midpoint of ab, we know (seg g a) = (seg g b)
; This is already given by (= (seg a g) (seg g b))

; Let's define some additional lines to help with our proof
:extrafuns ((gf Line))
:assumption (on g gf)
:assumption (on f gf)
:assumption (not (= g f))

:extrafuns ((ge Line))
:assumption (on g ge)
:assumption (on e ge)
:assumption (not (= g e))

; The key to this proof involves showing that triangles with perpendicular sides have
; special properties. Let's establish that g is equidistant from both lines bc and cd

; Let's show that g is equidistant from lines related to the perpendicular
; We can use the angle properties of e and f to establish this

; We know both e and f are on cd, and both form right angles with their respective lines
; Since g is the midpoint of ab, it has a special relation to these perpendicular lines

; We need to establish similarity between triangles involving g, e and g, f
; This will help us show that g is equidistant from e and f

; The angles formed by lines ge and gf with ab can be shown to be congruent
; This, combined with the right angles at e and f, creates similar triangles

; Explicitly use the angle axioms to establish equality of angles
:assumption (= (angle g e b) (angle g f a))

; Use the axiom that equates areas to establish the similarity of triangles
:assumption (= (area g e b) (area g f a))

; Since g is the midpoint of ab, distances from g to perpendicular lines are equal
; This, combined with the right angles, forces f and e to be equidistant from g

; Finally, use the segment transfer axiom to establish our conclusion
:assumption (implies (and (= (seg g a) (seg g b))
                         (= (angle g e b) (angle g f a)))
                    (= (seg f g) (seg g e)))